#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:100
#: 59275295b1ce4616949a5164e24d2db2
msgid ""
"There is a class of security vulnerabilities that can be fixed only by "
"microcode updates. If your computer or the CPU in it no longer receives "
"microcode updates (e.g., because it is too old), it may not be possible for "
"some of these vulnerabilities to be mitigated on your system, leaving you "
"vulnerable. For this reason, we recommend using Qubes OS on systems that are "
"still receiving microcode updates. Nonetheless, Qubes OS **can** run on "
"systems that no longer receive microcode updates, and such systems will "
"still offer significant security advantages over conventional operating "
"systems on the same hardware."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:102
#: bc63b87d29e94cc1a6fcaaa3e740abe7
msgid ""
"Intel maintains a `list <https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/support/"
"articles/000022396/processors.html>`__ of end-of-support dates for its "
"processors. However, this list seems to include only processors that are no "
"longer supported or will soon no longer be supported. Many newer Intel "
"processors are missing from this list. To our knowledge, Intel does not "
"announce end-of-support dates for its newer processors in advance, nor does "
"it have a public policy governing how long support will last."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:106
#: bec13f25de7f44009f658165fbf6d233
msgid ""
"Intel and AMD handle microcode updates differently, which has significant "
"security implications. On Intel platforms, microcode updates can typically "
"be loaded from the operating system. This allows the Qubes security team to "
"respond rapidly to new vulnerabilities by shipping microcode updates "
"alongside other security updates directly to users. By contrast, on AMD "
"client (as opposed to server) platforms, microcode updates are typically "
"shipped only as part of system firmware and generally cannot be loaded from "
"the operating system  [1]_. This means that AMD users typically must wait "
"for:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:108
#: 03ed54de1d9244d1acc977dda4be505b
msgid ""
"AMD to distribute microcode updates to original equipment manufacturers "
"(OEMs), original design manufacturers (ODMs), and motherboard manufacturers "
"(MB); and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:110
#: 85831ecde9b3441c95f6be2a44a6f96c
msgid ""
"The user’s OEM, ODM, or MB to provide a suitable BIOS or (U)EFI update for "
"the user’s system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:114
#: 82da0ba3b0f141fe9b04e3aa37a91a55
msgid ""
"Historically, AMD has often been slow to complete step (1), at least for its "
"client (as opposed to server) platforms  [2]_. In some cases, AMD has made "
"fixes available for its server platforms very shortly after a security "
"embargo was lifted, but it did not make fixes available for client platforms "
"facing the same vulnerability until weeks or months later. (A “security "
"embargo” is the practice of avoiding public disclosure of a security "
"vulnerability prior to a designated date.) By contrast, Intel has "
"consistently made fixes available for new CPU vulnerabilities across its "
"supported platforms very shortly after security embargoes have been lifted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:116
#: bdd047d068604c97905fdabf665e920e
msgid ""
"Step (2) varies by vendor. Many vendors fail to complete step (2) at all, "
"while some others take a very long time to complete it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:118
#: da423a657a114699a1f8adfd25daac0f
msgid ""
"The bottom line is that Qubes OS **can** run on AMD systems, and the Qubes "
"and Xen security teams do their best to provide security support for AMD "
"systems. However, without the ability to ship microcode updates, there is "
"only so much they can do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:122
#: 8a93c5c66fb344fcb3f8c8bd1a6be753
msgid ""
"Qubes **can** be installed on many systems that do not meet the recommended "
"requirements. Such systems will still offer significant security "
"improvements over traditional operating systems, since things like GUI "
"isolation and kernel protection do not require special hardware."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:124
#: a092408d0cc340b68830ed68a256cd0f
msgid ""
"Qubes **can** be installed on a USB flash drive or external disk, and "
"testing has shown that this works very well. A fast USB 3.0 flash drive is "
"recommended for this. (As a reminder, its capacity must be at least 32 GiB.) "
"Simply plug the flash drive into the computer before booting into the Qubes "
"installer from a separate installation medium, choose the flash drive as the "
"target installation disk, and proceed with the installation normally. After "
"Qubes has been installed on the flash drive, it can then be plugged into "
"other computers in order to boot into Qubes. In addition to the convenience "
"of having a portable copy of Qubes, this allows users to test for hardware "
"compatibility on multiple machines (e.g., at a brick-and-mortar computer "
"store) before deciding on which computer to purchase. (See :ref:`generating "
"and submitting HCL reports <user/hardware/how-to-use-the-hcl:generating and "
"submitting new reports>` for advice on hardware compatibility testing.) "
"Remember to change the devices assigned to your NetVM and USB VM if you move "
"between different machines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:126
#: 6d995dd6237547b1a8254e59d001582c
msgid ""
"You can check whether an Intel processor has VT-x and VT-d on `ark.intel.com "
"<https://ark.intel.com/content/www/us/en/ark.html#@Processors>`__. Note that "
"VT-d also requires chipset support. A processor with VT-d will not provide "
"IOMMU functionality if the chipset does not support it. Consult your "
"motherboard or system documentation to verify chipset compatibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:129
#: 4cf212057b90461a87068a0545802517
msgid ""
"There is an ``amd-ucode-firmware`` package, but it only contains microcode "
"for servers and outdated microcode for Chromebooks. Also, the `AMD security "
"website <https://www.amd.com/en/resources/product-security.html>`__ only "
"lists microcode as a mitigation for data center CPUs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:131
#: ef87cd682f354b55bb59a5f8c4d51de4
msgid ""
"As shown on `the AMD page for Speculative Return Stack Overflow <https://"
"www.amd.com/en/resources/product-security/bulletin/amd-sb-7005.html>`__, "
"updated AGESA™ firmware for AMD Ryzen™ Threadripper™ 5000WX Processors was "
"not available until 2024-01-11, even though the vulnerability became public "
"on 2023-08-08. AMD did not provide updated firmware for other client "
"processors until a date between 2023-08-22 to 2023-08-25."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/hardware/system-requirements.rst:133
#: b28ce8b36ccb461e91cbf1747b110da6
msgid ""
"For Zenbleed, firmware was not available until 2024 for most client parts, "
"even though server parts got microcode on 2023-06-06."
msgstr ""
